1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:03,700
Source Watch 4 turns YouTube into your research workspace.

2
00:00:03,700 --> 00:00:14,700
In a few clicks, prepare the transcript and a detailed prompt for ChatGPT
to assess the source's credibility and reliability, examine its evidence,
and fact-check its claims.

3
00:00:14,700 --> 00:00:17,112
Your working library stays on your computer.

4
00:00:17,234 --> 00:00:20,934
Open the supplied executable and follow the instructions.

5
00:00:21,184 --> 00:00:24,921
When Source Watch opens, your first step is to connect YouTube.

6
00:00:25,234 --> 00:00:29,446
On a fresh first launch, the welcome guide opens automatically.

7
00:00:29,484 --> 00:00:32,659
Choose Start setup to reach Setup and Preferences.

8
00:00:33,684 --> 00:00:37,197
Choose Connect YouTube to open Google's sign-in process.

9
00:00:38,684 --> 00:00:45,546
We connect first so Source Watch can load the subscriptions and playlists
belonging to the account you actually want to use.

10
00:00:45,546 --> 00:00:46,996
You sign in with Google.

11
00:00:46,996 --> 00:00:50,084
You do not enter your Google password into Source Watch.

12
00:00:50,201 --> 00:00:54,751
First, choose the Google account you use for the YouTube subscriptions you
want here.

13
00:00:54,751 --> 00:00:59,076
If Google asks you to sign in, complete that step on Google's own page.

14
00:00:59,084 --> 00:01:04,659
The account chooser may also ask you to choose between your personal
account and a YouTube brand account.

15
00:01:04,659 --> 00:01:08,309
Choose the identity containing the subscriptions you want to use.

16
00:01:08,884 --> 00:01:13,909
For this private test build, you may see a message that Google has not
verified the app.

17
00:01:13,909 --> 00:01:18,471
That means this build has not completed Google's public verification
process.

18
00:01:18,484 --> 00:01:22,972
Continue only if you are an invited tester using the expected Source Watch
build.

19
00:01:22,972 --> 00:01:27,322
If the application or request is unfamiliar, go back to safety.

20
00:01:31,484 --> 00:01:35,596
On the access screen, review the application name and the permissions.

21
00:01:35,596 --> 00:01:40,709
The current Source Watch connection requests both viewing and management
access to YouTube.

22
00:01:40,884 --> 00:01:48,084
On a first connection, if Google presents permission checkboxes, select the
requested permissions before choosing Continue.

23
00:01:48,084 --> 00:01:53,409
This account has connected before, so Google shows an existing-access
summary instead.

24
00:01:53,484 --> 00:01:58,596
Open the four services to inspect those permissions, choose Done, then
Continue.

25
00:01:59,484 --> 00:02:03,684
Viewing access lets Source Watch read your subscriptions and playlists.

26
00:02:04,484 --> 00:02:11,784
Management access allows supported actions such as updating a playlist or
unsubscribing when you explicitly request them.

27
00:02:11,884 --> 00:02:19,209
Google grants a broad capability here; Source Watch's confirmation controls
govern when the application uses it.

28
00:02:19,209 --> 00:02:21,134
It is not a read-only connection.

29
00:02:21,284 --> 00:02:23,909
After approval, return to Source Watch.

30
00:02:29,617 --> 00:02:32,892
Check the connected account and let your subscriptions load.

31
00:02:32,892 --> 00:02:35,542
A large subscription list can take a little time.

32
00:02:38,067 --> 00:02:43,467
Once the subscriptions have loaded, decide how much of your YouTube library
you want in Source Watch.

33
00:02:43,517 --> 00:02:48,242
You can bring in all of your subscribed channels and start with the
proposed categories.

34
00:02:48,242 --> 00:02:52,304
You can keep those suggested categories for a smaller selection of
channels.

35
00:02:52,304 --> 00:02:58,917
Or you can choose channels individually and change or create categories to
organize the dashboard your own way.

36
00:02:59,017 --> 00:03:02,292
For a large library, work in manageable pages.

37
00:03:02,292 --> 00:03:06,229
Select visible selects the eligible channels currently displayed.

38
00:03:06,229 --> 00:03:13,567
Check the count and categories, add the selected channels, then continue
through the remaining pages if you want your whole library.

39
00:03:14,517 --> 00:03:18,117
For a more focused start, select only the channels you want.

40
00:03:18,517 --> 00:03:24,017
The proposed categories are a starting point: keep them, remove them, or
add your own.

41
00:03:24,017 --> 00:03:27,267
Each selected channel needs a category before import.

42
00:03:27,284 --> 00:03:28,871
Then confirm the import.

43
00:03:31,284 --> 00:03:37,747
Your approved channels become monitored sources, and their categories shape
how you browse and filter your dashboard.

44
00:03:37,747 --> 00:03:41,959
You can refine that organization later in Sources and Categories.

45
00:03:42,134 --> 00:03:46,721
Refresh the feed to retrieve recent public uploads from your monitored
sources.

46
00:03:46,721 --> 00:03:49,771
Latest Long-form keeps longer videos together.

47
00:03:49,884 --> 00:03:51,322
Shorts have their own view.

48
00:03:53,884 --> 00:04:00,696
Narrow the feed by category, channel, status, or search text to find
material that matters to your question.

49
00:04:03,884 --> 00:04:08,497
The source score and rough priority are attention aids, not truth ratings.

50
00:04:08,497 --> 00:04:12,447
Let's choose one video and move directly into understanding it.

51
00:04:12,734 --> 00:04:15,534
Open Quick Brief for a fast first inspection.

52
00:04:16,184 --> 00:04:22,859
It extracts likely main points from the available material and flags simple
signals that may deserve attention.

53
00:04:22,984 --> 00:04:28,434
Check its basis: a transcript gives it more to work with than a title and
description alone.

54
00:04:28,484 --> 00:04:33,109
Quick Brief is an extractive brief, not a generative AI verdict.

55
00:04:33,109 --> 00:04:35,534
It helps you decide what to examine next.

56
00:04:35,734 --> 00:04:38,696
Here is one of Source Watch's most useful features.

57
00:04:38,717 --> 00:04:41,817
From the video, choose Send to Analysis Desk.

58
00:04:41,817 --> 00:04:44,880
You do not have to save it or build a selection first.

59
00:04:46,817 --> 00:04:51,329
The desk brings its available transcript and analysis controls together.

60
00:04:51,417 --> 00:04:58,780
Choose Copy Deep plus Transcript to copy the source material and a
structured critical-analysis prompt in one step.

61
00:04:58,817 --> 00:05:08,367
The prompt asks for more than a summary: reconstruct the argument, examine
the evidence, challenge weak points, and identify what needs verification.

62
00:05:08,417 --> 00:05:12,454
You can also choose a simpler prompt, or copy the transcript separately.

63
00:05:12,517 --> 00:05:16,867
Review the prepared text and adapt the question to what you need to
understand.

64
00:05:16,917 --> 00:05:24,117
If the available captions are unsuitable, V4 also provides caption-language
selection and manual transcript entry.

65
00:05:24,217 --> 00:05:26,405
Then click Open ChatGPT.

66
00:05:27,917 --> 00:05:31,129
It opens the ChatGPT website in your browser.

67
00:05:31,129 --> 00:05:38,092
If you are already signed in to ChatGPT in that browser, you can use that
signed-in account; otherwise, sign in there.

68
00:05:38,201 --> 00:05:43,976
On the ChatGPT page you just opened, paste the prepared material, review
it, and send it.

69
00:05:56,151 --> 00:05:59,713
Now we can ask: what is the video's strongest argument?

70
00:05:59,713 --> 00:06:01,151
What evidence supports it?

71
00:06:01,151 --> 00:06:05,351
Where does it rely on assumptions, rhetoric, or missing context?

72
00:06:05,351 --> 00:06:07,626
Which claims should we check before trusting them?

73
00:06:08,151 --> 00:06:10,988
Read the actual response alongside the source.

74
00:06:12,551 --> 00:06:18,851
Follow up with questions, ask for a counterargument, or identify what
evidence would change the conclusion.

75
00:06:24,051 --> 00:06:29,789
Source Watch prepares the material; ChatGPT performs the analysis after you
submit it.

76
00:06:29,789 --> 00:06:32,889
Neither the prompt nor the answer guarantees truth.

77
00:06:32,889 --> 00:06:38,339
The benefit is a clearer, deeper investigation—not an automatic stamp of
approval.

78
00:06:38,501 --> 00:06:40,101
Now keep what proved useful.

79
00:06:40,101 --> 00:06:45,263
Save marks a video locally, and Saved and Folders gives your retained
material a home.

80
00:06:45,751 --> 00:06:50,314
To work with several videos, click the selection box on each video you
want.

81
00:06:55,751 --> 00:06:59,888
Watch the selected count increase, then open Selected Videos.

82
00:07:00,451 --> 00:07:06,626
Choose a folder for the group, apply a batch action, or send the selected
videos to the Analysis Desk.

83
00:07:16,451 --> 00:07:19,926
These local actions are separate from YouTube playlist changes.

84
00:07:20,651 --> 00:07:25,801
When you choose a YouTube action, check its destination and confirmation
before applying it.

85
00:07:25,934 --> 00:07:28,134
You can also keep the analysis itself.

86
00:07:28,134 --> 00:07:34,184
Copy the useful result from ChatGPT back into Source Watch's Save analysis
result form.

87
00:07:36,284 --> 00:07:39,546
Add verification sources and dates, then save it.

88
00:07:42,284 --> 00:07:46,372
Save the investigation as a named workspace in Saved research.

89
00:07:49,284 --> 00:07:54,871
Keep the research question, conclusions, source videos and verification
notes together.

90
00:07:58,784 --> 00:08:02,109
You can reopen the record or export it as Markdown.

91
00:08:02,784 --> 00:08:08,672
Search My Research helps you find material again across saved transcripts,
notes and results.

92
00:08:12,784 --> 00:08:14,871
This return step is manual.

93
00:08:14,871 --> 00:08:19,521
Source Watch does not automatically retrieve your ChatGPT conversations.

94
00:08:19,634 --> 00:08:22,646
Discovery takes the question beyond one source.

95
00:08:22,684 --> 00:08:26,159
Choose More evidence, Challenge this, or Better source.

96
00:08:26,684 --> 00:08:32,922
Search the latest monitored feed, monitored history, or wider YouTube,
depending on the scope you need.

97
00:08:33,684 --> 00:08:37,946
Narrow the results and inspect the reasons and risks shown on the cards.

98
00:08:37,946 --> 00:08:42,709
These estimates guide your next search; they do not establish whether a
claim is true.

99
00:08:42,784 --> 00:08:46,421
Bring a promising result into the same analysis workflow.

100
00:08:46,534 --> 00:08:49,271
You are not limited to the sources you already know.

101
00:08:49,271 --> 00:08:54,796
Trending lets you choose a country and see YouTube's current most-popular
videos for that region.

102
00:08:54,984 --> 00:09:00,547
Narrow the list by a YouTube category, such as News and Politics or Science
and Technology.

103
00:09:02,684 --> 00:09:05,459
Switch countries to see how the results differ.

104
00:09:08,684 --> 00:09:12,096
Explore by Country goes beyond the popularity chart.

105
00:09:13,184 --> 00:09:19,259
Choose a country and topics such as economy, politics, technology, energy,
or culture.

106
00:09:21,684 --> 00:09:29,372
Adjust the language, recency and search depth to find videos, channels and
regional perspectives you might otherwise miss.

107
00:09:34,584 --> 00:09:39,159
This can help you investigate issues receiving attention in and around a
country.

108
00:09:39,184 --> 00:09:48,509
It is not a complete picture of what its population considers important:
popular videos are one signal, and country-based search can include outside
perspectives.

109
00:09:48,584 --> 00:09:53,159
Take an interesting result straight into Quick Brief or the Analysis Desk.

110
00:09:53,634 --> 00:09:56,371
Open Channel View and choose a monitored channel.

111
00:09:59,584 --> 00:10:03,697
Scroll through its videos to explore beyond the newest uploads.

112
00:10:05,084 --> 00:10:08,546
Enter a phrase in Search channel videos and run the search.

113
00:10:13,084 --> 00:10:18,722
The list narrows to matching material; date and duration filters can refine
it further.

114
00:10:18,722 --> 00:10:20,509
Check the archive's coverage.

115
00:10:20,509 --> 00:10:24,784
An empty or partial index is not the entire history of a channel.

116
00:10:24,884 --> 00:10:31,522
Where access is available, retrieve additional uploads and return to the
locally indexed material later.

117
00:10:31,834 --> 00:10:34,721
Choose a light or dark color scheme that suits you.

118
00:10:34,721 --> 00:10:38,084
The appearance changes; your research stays in place.

119
00:10:45,384 --> 00:10:49,659
Help remains available when you need a reminder of a page or workflow.

120
00:10:50,134 --> 00:10:55,609
You have connected YouTube, brought in your channels in the way you prefer,
and built your own research feed.

121
00:10:55,609 --> 00:11:01,084
From there, a few actions take you from a video to a deeper examination of
its argument.

122
00:11:01,084 --> 00:11:07,209
You can question the answer, explore other sources and countries, and keep
the useful results with your research.

123
00:11:07,284 --> 00:11:09,634
Start with one question that matters to you.

124
00:11:09,634 --> 00:11:14,909
Source Watch helps you organize the investigation; you remain responsible
for the judgment.

125
00:11:15,034 --> 00:11:18,396
As we said at the beginning, your working library stays with you.

126
00:11:18,396 --> 00:11:27,409
Sources, local saves, notes, cached transcripts and saved research are
stored on your computer, rather than in a separate Source Watch cloud
account.

127
00:11:27,484 --> 00:11:34,621
Connecting Google authorizes access to YouTube; it does not turn that
workspace into a Source Watch cloud library.

128
00:11:34,621 --> 00:11:38,921
The authorization token is also stored locally and should be protected.

129
00:11:38,984 --> 00:11:41,409
Local-first is not fully offline.

130
00:11:41,409 --> 00:11:44,884
YouTube supplies remote material and account services.

131
00:11:44,884 --> 00:11:50,397
Text you choose to submit to ChatGPT is handled by that service under its
own settings.

132
00:11:50,397 --> 00:11:53,597
Source Watch does not send your transcripts there automatically.

133
00:11:53,684 --> 00:11:55,946
Protect your computer and backups.

134
00:11:55,946 --> 00:12:00,996
A folder placed in OneDrive or another sync service may be copied by that
service.

135
00:12:00,996 --> 00:12:06,434
Local storage is not, by itself, a promise of encryption or a secure vault.
